Q7
The first difficulty was to get the B out of the phase because it wouldn’t show in the probability otherwise.
Having a complex with |0> and its conjugate with |1>, our idea was to sum them to create a cos that we could see.
Thus, we added an Hadamard gate before the U(B) one to create a superposition and have both complex terms.
To sum these terms, we wanted to change only the |1> by a |0> but this is impossible because it is not reversible.
Also, the result could not be unitary. The only way to see the cos is by it being a probability when we measure.
Therefore, we used another Hadamard gate after the U(B) to sum both exponential and have a cos in front of the |0>, and a sin in front of the |1>.
Thanks to the measurements, we should be able to know with which probability we obtain |0>, this probability being cos(B/100)2.
We implemanted this idea on the first qubit only at first to see what it would give but the results that we obtained were not satisfying since we had the impression the we always had P(0) = 1.
We decided to increase our precision by using AI tools which told use that the issue was that B being between 0 and π, B/100 is really small and thus cos(B/100) is nearly 1.
The AI assistant gave us the idea to do a GHZ circuit such as we get cos(15*B/100) = cos(3*B/20).
Then, we tried to optimize the results with AI by having the best post treatment to extract B for measurements.
At best, we obtained a score of 0.471

